window.docContents[139].resolve({"/Abstraction/Return/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Return":{"contents":"The return yields the control , which Seq-Ret and While-Ret propagate up to the call. The expression of return e is acceptable at the result type  of the enclosing body.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Abstraction","header":"5.4. Return","id":"/Abstraction/Return/#Core-C______--Abstraction--Return"},"/Design-restrictions/Objects/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Objects":{"contents":"In C++17 an object may live in a variable, and a class without a declared copy constructor gets one implicitly (N4659 §15.8.1 ¶6). The destructor of an automatic object runs implicitly when its block exits (N4659 §15.4 ¶12). So g++ accepts C c = *p;, which copies the object.Core C++ creates every object with new and reaches it by pointer or by reference. It never copies an object, and it rejects C c = *p; with the message that objects live behind pointers. A destructor runs only on delete, . There are no copy constructors, no RAII and no initialiser lists.The restriction leaves no implicit call. Every constructor and every destructor that runs appears in the program. The rules New of  and Delete of  show it in the derivation. An object is one record in the store, so two names of an object hold the same location. The exit of a block frees only the locations its declarations allocated, .\n\nIn C++17 a lambda may capture by reference. The call of its closure after the end of the lifetime of a captured entity is likely to have undefined behaviour (N4659 §8.1.5.2 ¶16). Each lambda also has a unique, unnamed closure type (N4659 §8.1.5.1 ¶1), which auto may hold.Core C++ admits only the capture [=], with copies of basic values and pointers that the body reads and never writes, . A lambda occurs only as the argument of new std::function<F>(λ), . The parser rejects [&] and auto f = [=]() -> int { return 1; };, which g++ both accept.The restriction keeps the locations of the enclosing block out of every closure, so a closure never reads a freed variable. A captured pointer still reaches its object in the store, and its dereference after a delete is error. The type of every lambda is the function type  of its std::function, so the type checker needs no closure types, .\n\nC++17 admits several base classes for one class, which g++ accepts in class C : public A, public B.Core C++ admits one public base class, and the parser rejects a second one, . Methods are defined inside the class.The restriction makes the bases of a class one chain, a list from the class to its root. The order of the fields, the constructors from the root down and the destructors from the class of the object up all follow that list, . A method call selects the method nearest the class in the list, so no member name is ambiguous between two bases, .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Design restrictions","header":"9.4. Objects","id":"/Design-restrictions/Objects/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Objects"}});